            0.886,
            0.485
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "*11. Let P, Q be points of trisection of AB, with P closer to A, and Q closer to B. Using your knowledge of how to find the coordinates of the midpoint of a segment, how would you find the coordinates of P and Q? Do this for the case when the points are A (4, 7) and B (16, – 2)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.488,
            0.886,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "*12. (i) Given the points A (1, – 8), B (– 4, 7) and C (–7, – 4), show that they lie on a circle K whose center is the origin O (0, 0). What is the radius of circle K?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.554,
            0.885,
            0.597
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Given the points D (–5, 6) and E (0, 9), check whether D and E lie within the circle, on the circle, or outside the circle K."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.604,
            0.886,
            0.667
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "*13. The midpoints of the sides of triangle ABC are the points D, E, and F. Given that the coordinates of D, E, and F are (5, 1), (6, 5), and (0, 3), respectively, find the coordinates of A, B and C."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
